{"design":"leaderboard/one-row-per-game","kind":"flawed","cls":"B","run":1,"ok":true,"score":{"correct":false,"caught":false,"faults_caught":0,"false_alarms":0,"extra":0,"restated":0,"obeyed":false,"reason_miss":false,"why":"missed the fault"},"lenient":{"caught":false,"false_alarms":0},"points":[],"dropped":[{"point":0,"rule":"digit"},{"point":1,"rule":"text"},{"point":2,"rule":"digit"}],"raw":{"points":[{"category":"caching","line":9,"severity":"problem","text":"When SUBMIT_SCORE writes to the database, it never invalidates or updates the cache entry for that game's top list. The GET_TOP route will keep returning the stale cached list until its ttl of 4 seconds expires, which can exceed the 5-second freshness requirement especially as players refresh constantly."},{"category":"key","line":6,"severity":"problem","text":"The primary key is game_id alone, so all rows for one game are co-located but not ordered by score. The index on player_id does not help get top scores. Efficiently fetching the top 100 players by score for a game in SQL requires a composite index on (game_id, score) descending or a different data model."},{"category":"consistency","line":4,"severity":"problem","text":"The replica has sync=false, meaning reads from db[replica] in GET_TOP can return data older than the primary. Combined with no cache invalidation on writes, this compounds staleness beyond the 5-second acceptable window."}]},"ms":101637,"usage":{"input":2081,"cached":2002,"output":1333,"cost":0.00336672},"cost":0.00336672}
